<title>YUGOSLAVIA: Yugoslav May trade gap $124mln vs $58mln in April.</title>
<headline>Yugoslav May trade gap $124mln vs $58mln in April.</headline>
<dateline>BELGRADE 1997-06-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Federal Statistics Office reported the following economic indicator details.</p>
<p> YUGOSLAV TRADE	    MAY	   APRIL	  MAY 96</p>
<p> Exports million USD   171.0	   184.0	   120.9</p>
<p> Imports		   295.0	   242.0	   243.5</p>
<p> Balance		  -124.0	  - 58.0	  -122.6</p>
<p>CUMULATIVE FIGURES (JANUARY TO MAY)</p>
<p> Exports million USD   862.0	   677.0	   596.6</p>
<p> Imports		 1,709.0	 1,330.0	 1,246.0</p>
<p> Balance		  -847.0	  -653.0	   649.4</p>
<p>NOTES -- May year-on-year exports rose 25.7 percent, while imports dropped 9.6 perent, the report said.</p>
<p>Cumulative January-May exports were 39.0 percent up and imports 25.5 percent up on the same five-month period 1996.</p>
<p>Yugoslavia's 1996 trade gap stood at $2.26 billion, with total exports worth around $1.842 and imports $4.102 billion.</p>
<p>Data refers to Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, comprising Serbia and Montenegro.</p>
<p>-- Amra Kevic, Belgrade newsroom + 381 11 222 4305</p>
</text>
